Ohle Claussen <ohle.claussen@ds.mpg.de> barked:
> George Gibb wrote:

>> Knights, Cavemen,and Samauri seem a lot like Barbs and Valks, but I
>> guess I could try em, they seem a bit boring though.  Is there
>> anything to differentiate them?

> Vanilla Cavemen are notoriously dull. Kni and Sam may seem quite
> similar, but there are differences. Both are tough melee fighters
> from the start, both have codes of conduct (with mostly negligible
> consequences if you break them). The Sam starts with a yumi and ya,
> and a multishot bonus for those, so you've got ranged combat covered
> for the start. The knight has a mount, a lance that you can apply
> from two spaces away, and can jump. Also, later on knights are better
> spellcasters, especially with the quest artifact when casting attack
> spells. If you're good at keeping pets, you might find a mounted
> knight more interesting than the fairly standard gameplay of the
> samurai.

One thing to add about the knight and his pony: Don't try to mount it  
right away, wait until you've got some more Hp. Slipping and dying  
because of it is too easy.

(And remember that it doesn't eat meat. Also, IIRC, the big versions of  
horses (warhorse?) are tough enough to start biting shopkeeres, but not  
tough enough to win.)

